{"type":"route","meta":{"status":200,"headers":{"cache-control":"public, max-age=3600","content-type":"text/markdown; charset=utf-8","x-robots-tag":"noindex","x-next-cache-tags":"_N_T_/layout,_N_T_/md/layout,_N_T_/md/[...slug]/layout,_N_T_/md/[...slug]/route,_N_T_/md/blog/collect-wedding-rsvps-without-whatsapp-chaos"}},"body":"# How to collect wedding RSVPs without three hundred WhatsApp replies\n\n> Why Indian weddings are hard to count, what to ask for instead of a yes or no, and how to get a headcount the caterer can actually work from.\n\n- Source: http://localhost:3000/blog/collect-wedding-rsvps-without-whatsapp-chaos\n- Author: The Amantrika Team, Amantrika\n- Published: 4 July 2026\n- Category: Planning\n- Tags: RSVP, Planning, Guest list\n- Reading time: 4 min\n\n---\nCounting guests at an Indian wedding is harder than at most weddings, for a reason that has nothing to do with technology: invitations are extended to families, attendance is decided by families, and the number changes until the week of the event.\n\nNo RSVP system fixes that. A good one just stops you from doing the arithmetic by hand.\n\n## Why the WhatsApp method breaks down\n\nIt works fine up to about forty guests. Past that it fails in specific, predictable ways.\n\n- **Replies arrive across a dozen chats and three family groups.** There is no list; there is a scroll.\n- **\"We'll definitely come!\" is not a number.** How many is \"we\"? Are the children coming? For which functions?\n- **Responses to the wrong person.** The bride's mother gets half of them, the groom's cousin the other half, and neither has the whole picture.\n- **Late changes overwrite nothing.** A \"make that four instead of six\" sent three weeks after the original message is simply gone.\n- **You cannot tell who has not replied**, because absence of a reply looks identical to a reply you scrolled past.\n\n### The cost of getting this wrong\nCatering is usually priced per plate with a guaranteed minimum. Over-count and you pay for empty seats; under-count and there is a real, visible shortage at the reception. This is the most expensive spreadsheet at the wedding.\n\n## Ask for the right things\n\nThe single biggest improvement is not the tool. It is what you ask. A yes/no question produces answers you cannot cater from.\n\n| Ask this | Instead of | Because |\n|---|---|---|\n| How many adults and how many children? | How many guests? | Children's plates are often priced differently, and \"guests\" gets interpreted inconsistently |\n| Which functions will you attend? | Are you coming? | Different functions, different catering, different headcounts |\n| Name of the person responding | *(nothing)* | Otherwise you get a count with no idea whose family it is |\n| Phone number | *(nothing)* | You will need to reach someone about arrival timings |\n| Arrival date, for out-of-town guests | *(nothing)* | Drives hotel blocks and airport pickups |\n| Meal preference, if you're catering separately | *(nothing)* | Only ask if it changes what the caterer does; otherwise it's noise |\n\nKeep it to what you will act on. Every extra field costs you completed responses, and a field you never look at costs you responses for nothing.\n\n## Make responding take fifteen seconds\n\nGuests do not abandon RSVP forms because they are undecided. They abandon them because the form asked for eight things while they were standing in a queue.\n\n### Put the form in the invitation itself\nNot a link to a separate site, not an email address. The guest is already looking at the invitation; the form should be the next thing they scroll to.\n### Pre-fill everything you already know\nIf you sent a personalised link, their name and phone number should already be filled in. What remains is a headcount and a function or two — genuinely a few seconds.\n### Don't require an account\nNo login, no OTP, no app. Every step between the guest and the submit button costs you responses, and the elderly relatives you most need a count for are the first to drop off.\n### Confirm on screen, immediately\n\"Thank you, we have you down for four\" prevents the double-submission that inflates your count.\n\n## Chase the non-responders, not everyone\n\nThe reminder that works is targeted. A message to the whole list irritates the two hundred people who already replied and teaches them to ignore the next one.\n\nA workable schedule:\n\n- **On sending:** expect a burst of responses in the first 48 hours. This is most of your eventual total.\n- **Three to four weeks before:** a reminder to people who have not responded. This is the one that recovers the most.\n- **Ten days before:** a final, short message to the remaining non-responders. Give a deadline and mean it.\n- **After the deadline:** stop asking and estimate. Some families will simply arrive.\n\n### The estimate that saves you\nTrack how many invited guests have not responded and assume a share of them attend anyway. Your caterer has seen this a hundred times and will have a number for your region — ask them rather than guessing.\n\n## What to do with the responses\n\nA list of names is not a plan. Before the week of the wedding, turn the responses into four things:\n\n1. **A headcount per function**, which is what the caterer is waiting for.\n2. **An out-of-town list with arrival dates**, which drives hotel rooms and pickups.\n3. **A contact list for the day itself** — the people someone will need to call when a family cannot find the venue.\n4. **A seating or table plan**, if you are doing one, built from confirmed numbers rather than the invitation list.\n\nIf your RSVP responses cannot be exported to a spreadsheet, you will end up retyping them at the worst possible moment. Check that before you start collecting.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n### How far in advance should the RSVP deadline be?\n\nTwo to three weeks before the wedding for most caterers, though ask yours — many need final numbers earlier than couples expect, and some accept adjustments up to 72 hours before.\n\n### What do I do about guests who never respond?\n\nAssume a portion never will and plan for it. A targeted reminder to only the non-responders, about ten days before, recovers most of them. Chasing the whole list annoys the people who already replied.\n\n### Should I ask for a headcount per function?\n\nYes, if different functions have different catering. A guest attending the sangeet and the reception but not the mehndi is common, and a single yes/no cannot express that.\n"}